# v11 -> v11_v1. PARS: (paragraph prefix, new text); {n;m} = citation control. DELETE: paragraphs removed. IMAGES: drawing index -> PNG.
PARS = [
("Abstract—", """Abstract—Decision support systems (DSSs) are systems of systems. Most decide inside a closed decision space, a set of rules fixed before the incident. Outside this set, the system cannot respond. This article presents an architecture with an open decision space. It defines the invariants of the space, a verified set of physical effects from which new interventions are composed, and a chain of gates. A generative model proposes new rules and blended or composed interventions. A proposal enters the rule base only after deterministic checks and a paired forecast test on reseeded futures. A simulation core holds the hazard state. A decision core reads it through confidence-rated observations. The decision core has a closed layer, a sparse Takagi-Sugeno rule base that tunes itself online, and an open layer that proposes and verifies new objects. The approach is applied to wildfire response and tested on 150 paired worlds in 5 scenario families. Compared with the closed layer alone, the open layer reduces burned area by 40%, affected population by 70%, and committed response by 28%. It raises the share of extinguished incidents from 52 to 75%. The decision core is distributed and suits real-time operation. Each agent reasons only over its own region, so computing cost grows slowly with the number of regions."""),

("DSSs exist for parts", """DSSs exist for parts of this task {5;6}. Spread simulators forecast fire behavior from fuel, terrain, and weather {7;8}. Allocation tools assign crews and aircraft against a forecast {9;10}. Integrated platforms bring these parts together for use in the field {4;11;12}. However, field studies of these tools measure how widely they are used, not whether the decisions taken with them are better {13;14}. A critical review of DSS research reports the same pattern across the field. Many systems are built, and few are shown to help {15}. DisasterAware is therefore evaluated on the second question only. Its benefit is measured as the change in the outcome of the same incidents, with and without the system, as reported in Section V."""),

("Three structural limits", """Three structural limits exist across these systems. First, the alternatives are predefined. A rule base, an optimisation model, or a menu of tactics fixes the set of orders before the incident. Hence, the system can only rank the alternatives it is given. It cannot produce an order that was not written before the incident, even when the incident requires one {11;16;17}. Decision analysis has long identified the generation of alternatives as the weakest step of the process {18;19;20}. The methods proposed for it either enumerate variations of a fixed model {21;22} or depend on the recall of the operator under time pressure {23}. Second, uncertainty is representable only through prior distributions or belief intervals. Real-time sensing cannot supply them {24;25}. Furthermore, the reasoning behind a recommendation is exposed, if at all, through post-hoc attribution {26}. Third, integrated platforms embed the forecast and the decision logic in one tool {4;11}. Therefore, neither is validated on its own, and the benefit of a decision mechanism cannot be separated from the quality of the forecast behind it. In other words, the decision space of current systems is closed. It is fixed before the incident, and it cannot be verified or extended during it."""),

("DisasterAware answers these limits", """DisasterAware answers these three limits with an architecture whose central contribution is an open decision space. The set of orders the system may issue is not fixed in advance. It grows during the incident, under verification by the hazard model. This answers the first limit. The architecture separates two cores. One of them is a simulation core which owns the hazard state and is validated against satellite records. The other is a decision core which reads the state through confidence-rated observations. This separation answers the third limit, since each core is validated on its own and the benefit of a decision is measured on the same forecast. The second limit is answered in two steps. Every observation carries a confidence, and the confidence gates every concept that depends on it, so uncertainty is handled without a distribution. Moreover, every order is written over named decision concepts, so the reason for a recommendation is read from the rule that produced it rather than reconstructed afterwards. In detail, the decision core has two layers, namely the closed and the open layer. The closed layer is a self-tuning fuzzy doctrine. Bounded observation features aggregate into decision concepts, a sparse rule base maps the concepts to intervention families, and an evolving mechanism tunes consequents and term boundaries while the vocabulary stays fixed. The open layer sits on top of it. When the standing decision falls short, a pretrained large language model (LLM) proposes a new object. The object is a new rule, a blend of existing interventions, or an intervention composed from physical effects the simulator can execute. Nothing enters the doctrine unless it passes a chain of algebraic gates and a paired forecast test on independently seeded futures."""),

("This article asks what the open layer", """The value of the open layer is measured by paired comparison. The same fires are replayed under three configurations, namely without decision support, with the closed layer alone, and with both layers. The benefit of opening the decision space is therefore the difference observed on identical worlds. The article makes three contributions. First, the open decision space is defined as an architectural object. It is the set of orders a system may issue, together with the rules under which that set may be extended. The invariants this object preserves are stated and proved for the gate chain of Section IV. Second, the extension mechanism is specified to the level of its grammar and geometry. A proposal is a tuple over a closed basis of verified effects, sectors, and distance bands, and the simulator interprets it through a fixed operator algebra. Thus, the space is closed at effects and open at interventions. Third, the architecture is measured end to end on an independently validated hazard model, over paired worlds drawn from several scenario families. Every proposal, gate verdict, and admitted object is logged. Therefore, the question of what was learned can be answered object by object rather than only in aggregate. The contribution is architectural rather than algorithmic. The concept-driven inference and the generative proposer are replaceable components, and the invariants are properties of the gates. The remainder of the article is organised as follows. Section II reviews related work. Section III presents the architecture and Section IV the decision core. Section V reports the results, Section VI discusses them, and Section VII concludes."""),

("Decision analysis has long assumed", """Decision analysis has long assumed that the set of alternatives is given before evaluation begins {32;33}. Arbel and Tong {18} identified option generation as a neglected phase that precedes choice. Colorni and Tsoukias {19;34} formalized the construction of the alternative set as a recursion of decision problems. Hofstede {20} observed that planning systems which hard-wire the open aspects of a problem are not adopted. Cognitive evidence points the same way. Option generation in ill-structured problems depends on ideation beyond memory retrieval. Furthermore, generating more options raises the quality of the best option while it lowers the mean quality of the set {35}. Operators build courses of action from recognized patterns rather than from enumerated menus {23}. These works motivate a DSS that generates alternatives during operation. They also set the condition under which generation is safe. In other words, broad generation must be paired with strict, external evaluation."""),

("Operational assessments arrive", """Operational assessments arrive as graded, linguistic judgments. Their formal counterpart is partial membership {52}. Domain knowledge enters as rules of the canonical conjunctive form. Zero-order TS inference turns rule activations into a crisp intensity as a firing-strength weighted average of constant consequents {39}. The choice is architectural. Candidates are judged inside shadow forecasts that run many times per incident, so centroid integration would be too costly. Moreover, the outputs are intensity commands, and adaptation needs numeric consequents with predictable effect while antecedents stay linguistic. Applying inference directly over a rich observation vector is combinatorially infeasible. In fact, ten features with five terms each span nearly ten million antecedent combinations. A conceptual middle layer reduces this to five decision concepts. Each concept answers exactly once one of the questions operators ask {3;23}. Thus, the antecedent space shrinks to 3125 combinations, and only the active subset is instantiated. Furthermore, the concepts double as the vocabulary in which every recommendation is explained."""),

("Fig. 1 presents DisasterAware", """Fig. 1 presents DisasterAware as an SoS with five kinds of participant. External providers supply weather, imagery, terrain, and resource status. A simulation core owns the hazard state. N local decision agents are each responsible for one region. A coordination layer allocates capacity without performing inference. An operator supervises through a dashboard. Participants meet only at declared interfaces. The simulation core is authoritative. Cells carry burning status, fuel mass, an intensity proxy, and time since ignition, and no other participant may write this state. The decision agents read derived observations. The dashboard admits only reversible, input-level actions such as pause, rollback, ignition injection, and resource changes."""),

("Fig. 1. DisasterAware as an SoS.", """Fig. 1. DisasterAware as an SoS. The simulation core owns the hazard state and exposes it only through the observation function; the decision core reads confidence-rated observations, decides in two layers, and acts only through the intervention field. The operator supervises through bounded, reversible inputs."""),

("The proposer is a pretrained LLM", """The proposer is a pretrained LLM used in context only, with no task-specific training. It receives a deterministic situation summary holding the gated concept activations, the rules that fire and their strengths, the deficit, the resource state, and the vocabulary of sectors and distance bands. It returns exactly one object in one of three forms:"""),

("Every proposal is untrusted", """Every proposal is untrusted by construction and passes the gate chain of Fig. 4. Four algebraic gates run without a simulation step. G1 checks canonical form. G2 checks vocabulary, ranges, and duplication. G2c checks relevance. The proposed rule must fire on the current reading. G2d checks pool economy. The requested capacity must not exceed the pool. Four simulation gates follow. G3 and G4 demand that the forecast physical cost with the proposal strictly fall below the forecast without it on two independently seeded rollouts (12). G5 requires a package, a rule carrying a new object, to clear an additional margin δ. G5b requires the package to beat its own rule stripped of the new object, as in (13). Hence, a package is rejected when its gain belongs to the ordinary orders written beside the new object. A rejection returns the gate name and both forecast values to the proposer. The proposer may revise once, and a second rejection ends the attempt. An admitted object enters the rule base, is tagged with its origin, and is logged with its gate values. Therefore, every later order traces to the deficit that triggered it and to the test that admitted it."""),

("Adaptation runs only on demand", """Adaptation runs only on demand. Hence, the volume of trials measures how often the standing decision fell short, and the admission rate measures how strictly the gates filtered the responses. Over the 150 worlds the closed-layer mechanisms inside DisasterAware kept 451 of 2093 consequent-step trials and 469 of 1411 resolution trials. The open layer received 710 proposals, one per call with a single revision, and admitted 309 (44 percent). Fig. 9 shows where the rejections fell. In all, 227 proposals passed the forecast tests but failed the package margin G5, 155 did not improve the forecast at G3, 12 failed the second seed at G4, and 7 were stopped by the syntactic gates. In other words, the model writes well-formed objects, and the simulator decides. Admission depends on the form of the proposal. Macros that blend existing families were admitted in 80 percent of 71 cases, composed interventions in 46 percent of 400, and plain rules over the existing vocabulary in 28 percent of 239. Thus, the objects that extend the vocabulary pass more often than new rules inside it."""),

("Fig. 9. (a) The 788 proposals", """Fig. 9. (a) The 710 proposals by kind and verdict. (b) Admitted objects per scenario family, with the number of worlds (of 30) in which at least one was admitted."""),

("What was written falls into", """What was written falls into a small number of tactics, each absent from the seed doctrine, as shown in Fig. 10 and Fig. 11. The 67 admitted rules order retardant drops (45), containment lines (39), water drafting (38), and suppression effort (35) far more than the seed base does. Retardant drop and water drafting are not ordered by any seed rule. Hence, the open layer added aerial attack to the doctrine. The 57 macros reduce to two archetypes. Interface hardening is asset protection with a containment line at 0.7 to 0.8 (14 admissions). The downwind backburn line is a containment line with suppression effort and, at times, resource deployment (15 admissions). The 185 composed interventions, 159 distinct clause sets, are dominated by two geometries. The asset collar is retardant coating of the threatened assets and of the populated cells within two to three cells before the fire arrives (26 admissions in its plain form, 42 with variants). The head-flank retardant band is coating of the head within six cells and of the flanks within three, with or without water drafting at the fire (41 in its two plain forms, 56 with variants). Backfire, wetting, and evacuation clauses are rare and enter only where the forecast rewards them. Fig. 3 shows one such object with its gate values."""),

("Fig. 10. What the open layer wrote.", """Fig. 10. What the open layer wrote. (a) Families ordered by the five seed rules against the 67 admitted rules. (b) Families bundled together in the 57 admitted macros."""),

("Every world starts from the same", """Every world starts from the same five seed rules, and the proposer has no memory across worlds. Hence, an object that recurs in several worlds was rediscovered, not copied. Counting every variant that contains the defining clauses, the asset collar was admitted in 42 distinct worlds, the head-flank retardant band in 51, the interface-hardening macro in 18, and the downwind backburn line in 28. Recurrence across independent worlds is the first evidence that what the open layer learns is structural rather than incidental."""),

("The realized outcome confirms", """The realized outcome confirms the forecast that admitted the objects, as shown in Fig. 12. In the 21 worlds with no admission, DisasterAware and the closed layer end within 16 ha of each other on average, and 11 of them are identical. The margin of Section V-C comes from the 129 worlds with at least one admission, where the mean saving is 32.2 ha. Moreover, the saving grows with the number of admissions in the world. This attribution is observational, since the admitted set is not assigned at random. The causal version, replaying each world with one admitted object removed, is left to future work. The single world in Fig. 12(c) shows the mechanism at the scale of one fire. It is an interface world in which the closed layer ends at 74 ha and DisasterAware at 6 ha. The curves separate at minute 14, when one composed intervention coating the threatened assets and the head was admitted."""),

("Against the eight criteria", """Against the eight criteria, the picture is as follows. Predictive fidelity rests on four blind hindcasts with front agreement of 86 to 94 percent. Decision quality follows from the paired comparison, 82 percent less burned area than no action and 40 percent less than the closed layer, better in 128 of 150 paired worlds, with the lowest cost in every family. Timeliness is met with 274 ms per decision and about two seconds of proposer time per cycle. Scalability is met with at most 1.29 times the cost for sixteen times the agents. Robustness is met with a loss of 7 percent under degraded observation while the configuration remains the best tested. Uncertainty handling is structural, since every feature carries a confidence that gates its concept. Adaptability shows in 309 admitted objects that recur across worlds. Explainability shows in every edit, rejection, and generated object being logged with its trigger, gate, and consuming rule. Four criteria are met in full and four on the axis measured here. In detail, the hindcast base covers four fires, outage and noise are tested at one level only, operator-facing interpretability is not evaluated, and uncertainty is carried into the decision but not propagated as an ensemble."""),

("Three lessons transfer", """Three lessons transfer beyond wildfire. First, separating an authoritative hazard model from the decision layer is what makes both testable. The model was validated against satellite records with no decision logic in the loop. The decision layers were then measured against each other on the same worlds. Hence, the benefit of opening the decision space is a paired difference rather than an absolute score. Second, a vocabulary of orders that grows at run time is safe only when admission is decided by gates that do not trust the proposer. More than half of the proposals were rejected, most of them at the package margin after they had passed the forecast tests. That selectivity is why the admitted objects could be fielded and why the outcome improved rather than merely changed. Third, the two layers divide the work by cost and determinism rather than by capability. The moves of the closed layer are deterministic, cheap, and numerous, 3504 trials against 710, and they finish the fires the doctrine already holds. The proposals of the open layer are expensive and not bitwise reproducible, and they reach the fires the doctrine does not hold. A single generative mechanism could in principle write the tuning edits as rules, at roughly four times the model calls of this campaign. However, keeping the deterministic layer underneath minimizes the number of cycles in which the non-reproducible component acts. It also keeps the ledger able to say which change came from tuning and which from generation."""),

("This article presented DisasterAware", """This article presented DisasterAware, a distributed decision support architecture for wildfire response built on two separated cores and two decision layers. The closed layer is a self-tuning fuzzy doctrine over five decision concepts and six intervention families. The open layer lets a pretrained LLM extend that doctrine with rules, blended interventions, and interventions composed from verified physical effects. A gate chain preserves no-harm, life-safety invariance, coverage, and monotone descent whatever the proposer. On an independently validated core, 150 paired worlds in five scenario families were replayed with no decision support, with the closed layer alone, and with both layers. The open layer admitted 309 of 710 proposals. It burned 29.9 ± 7.2 ha less per world than the closed layer and was better in 128 of 150 worlds. It raised the share of incidents extinguished from 52 to 75 percent and left 70 percent fewer people affected. This was achieved with a smaller committed response, no growth in rule count, and about two seconds of proposer time per decision cycle. The admitted objects recur across independent worlds and reduce to a small set of tactics the seed doctrine did not contain, namely aerial attack, the asset collar, the head-flank retardant band, interface hardening, and the downwind backburn line. Four studies follow. A leave-one-out replay will attribute the gain to each admitted object. A withheld-knowledge study will remove a known tactic from the doctrine and measure its rediscovery. A tabletop exercise will put fire officers in the loop. A transfer study will keep the decision core and replace the hazard model with another spreading hazard."""),
]
